## Callback API

`subagents.workflow(function(ctx) ... end, opts?)` returns a field-read-only
workflow record immediately. The callback runs in a background coroutine and
must return a string. Save the returned record or its `id`; completion wakes
you with that string, and `subagents.workflows[id]` holds per-agent detail. Do not poll
or sleep waiting for completion; do other work or end your turn.

```lua
return subagents.workflow(function(ctx)
  local result = ctx:agent{
    name="inspect", system_prompt="Inspect code. Read-only.",
    prompt="Inspect the current workspace and summarize its structure.",
  }:await()
  return result.output or result.error or result.status
end)
```

- `ctx:agent{name=..., profile|system_prompt|id=..., prompt=..., model?,
  reasoning?, output?}` returns a handle. Each name is nonempty and unique
  within this workflow, even across stages. Selector/prompt/model rules match
  `subagents.run` (see `.delegation`).
- `handle:await()` returns one settled result. `ctx:await(handles, "all")`
  returns results in input order. `ctx:await(handles, "first")` returns one
  settled result and an array of remaining handles (not automatically cancelled).
  Default mode is `"all"`; repeated awaits reuse settled results.
- Results carry `status`, `output`, `error`, and `id` when available. Check
  `status` before consuming output. Child/spawn failures are result values;
  invalid arguments, duplicate names, callback errors, or exceeded limits fail
  the workflow. Unawaited children are drained before the workflow settles.
- `opts.profiles={{name=..., system_prompt=..., description?}, ...}` provides
  workflow-local profiles that shadow discovered ones only inside that workflow.
  Use `ctx:agent` model/reasoning overrides rather than inline profile fields.
- At most 32 child starts per namespace workflow; host concurrency defaults to
  five (layered `[subagents].max_concurrent` can override it). Excess concurrent
  work queues. The callback has a 10,000,000-instruction guard.
- Callbacks cannot call `tools.*`, `panto.ext.call_tool`, or
  `panto.ext.start_tool`: those require the registered top-level tool-handler
  coroutine. Run tool calls in the top-level std.lua script, not a callback.

## Records and lifecycle

Workflow records expose `id`, `status`, `result`, `error`, `agents`, and
`:cancel()`. `agents[name]` exposes the named child's `name`, `status`, `output`,
`error`, `id`, and `:cancel()`. Fields are read-only; cancellation requests
return true only while running. Status is `running`, `completed`, `failed`, or
`cancelled`. Workflow child record outputs are text; structured handle outputs
are decoded Lua values. Direct delegations live in `subagents.agents`, not
`subagents.workflows`.

Records render progress boards when returned from std.lua. Settled records and
IDs persist across rebuilds/resumes. Ordinary primary turn endings leave work
running; interruption cancels ordinary work. Command-started workflows survive
primary interruption. Session switches are refused while work runs; forced
teardown cancels it. Completion sends a turn carrying the agent output or workflow result.
The namespace and all these documentation topics are withheld from child lanes.

## Structured one-turn workers

Pass `output={name="emit_result", description=..., schema=<JSON Schema table>}`
to `ctx:agent` (`name` defaults to `emit_result`). The child must call that
synthetic output tool; prose alone or missing/invalid structured output fails
the child. Successful handle `.output` is the decoded value. Workers are
one-shot, not resumable; do not combine `id` with `output`.

```lua
return subagents.workflow(function(ctx)
  local r = ctx:agent{
    name="classify", system_prompt="Use the result tool to classify input.",
    prompt="Is 2 even?",
    output={schema={type="object", required={"even"},
      properties={even={type="boolean"}}}},
  }:await()
  if r.status ~= "completed" then return r.error or r.status end
  return json.encode(r.output)
end)
```

Validation covers `type`, `enum`, `required`, `properties`,
`additionalProperties=false`, `items`, `minItems`/`maxItems`,
`minLength`/`maxLength`, and `minimum`/`maximum`; other keywords are ignored.

## Discovered fixed graphs

The `subagents.workflow` TOOL accepts `{profile=..., prompt=...}` and returns a
workflow id, not a record. In std.lua use `tools.subagents__workflow{...}`;
`subagents.workflow(...)` is the callback API, not that tool. The tool is omitted
only when the discovered workflow inventory is empty; invalid entries still
count, so the tool can explain their load errors. `/workflow:<name>` commands
are registered only for valid profiles. Each dependent step receives its predecessors'
labeled outputs; terminal or explicitly selected steps become the report in
`subagents.workflows[id].result`.
